# Normalise line endings so an edit made on any platform produces a diff of the
# lines that actually changed.
#
# Ten files in this repo were CRLF while everything else was LF, so a one-word
# change to one of them could render as a whole-file diff — which is how a
# 162-line diff for a single word slipped through review in #218.
#
# The usual culprit is a read-then-write round trip. Python text mode reads
# with universal newlines, so read_text() turns CRLF into "\n", and write_text()
# then emits "\n" — neither call rewrites anything on its own, but together they
# silently drop the CRLF unless both are passed newline="". Several editors do
# the same on save.
#
# text=auto: let git detect text vs binary, and store text as LF in the repo.
# eol=lf:    check text out as LF everywhere, including Windows. This repo has
#            no Windows-only tooling that needs CRLF, and pinning the working
#            tree too is what stops the churn coming back.
* text=auto eol=lf
